  "id": "COMPOUND:33008 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isofurcatain exerts its biological effects through a series of molecular interactions involving the NQO1 protein, which in turn targets Phosphodiesterase 6C. This pathway is supported by multiple independent connections, all of which point to NQO1 as a central mediator. The NQO1 protein is targeted by three distinct compounds—5,7-Dihydroxy-2-(4-Hydroxy-3-Methoxyphenyl)-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-Trihydroxy-6-[[(3R,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-Trihydroxy-6-Methyloxan-2-Yl]Oxymethyl]Oxan-2-Yl]Oxychromen-4-One, 5-Hydroxy-3-Amino-2-Aceto-1,4-Naphthoquinone, and 2',6'-Dihydroxy-4'-Geranyloxyacetophenone—each of which is linked to Phosphodiesterase 6C. This indicates that Isofurcatain may act through a common mechanism involving NQO1, which in turn modulates Phosphodiesterase 6C. The evidence suggests that the relationship between Isofurcatain and Phosphodiesterase 6C is mediated by NQO1, with the three target compounds serving as potential ligands that interact with NQO1 to influence Phosphodiesterase 6C. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of NQO1 as a critical node in the molecular network that connects Isofurcatain to its target enzyme.",
